body:
### Version

v24.22.0, v24.22.1, v26.9.0 – v26.12.1 (regression; last good: v24.21.0, v26.8.2, v25.9.0, v22.23.4)

### Platform

Linux (official `node:*-alpine` Docker images, linux/amd64). Also seen in production on the same images.

### Subsystem

http

### What steps will reproduce the bug?

A chunked `http.ServerResponse` stops sending data and never emits `'drain'` after the first backpressure cycle when the written strings contain non-ASCII characters. ASCII-only data is fine.

```js
import http from 'node:http';

const nonAscii = process.argv[2]; // try "x" (works) and "—" (hangs)

const server = http.createServer(async (req, res) => {
  let writes = 0;
  let drainWaits = 0;
  const watchdog = setTimeout(() => {
    console.log(process.version, `char=${JSON.stringify(nonAscii)}`,
      `STUCK: writes=${writes} drainWaits=${drainWaits}`,
      `writableLength=${res.writableLength} hwm=${res.writableHighWaterMark}`,
      `corked=${res.writableCorked} socketWritableLength=${res.socket.writableLength}`);
    process.exit(1);
  }, 2500);

  for (let i = 0; i < 4000; i++) {
    const pieces = [(i ? ',' : '') + '{"id":"row_' + i + '","note":"Accrued payroll ', nonAscii, ' March","amount":' + i * 1.5 + '}'];
    for (const piece of pieces) {
      writes++;
      if (!res.write(piece)) {
        drainWaits++;
        await new Promise((resolve) => res.once('drain', resolve));
      }
    }
  }
  clearTimeout(watchdog);
  res.end();
  console.log(process.version, `char=${JSON.stringify(nonAscii)}`, `DONE writes=${writes} drainWaits=${drainWaits}`);
});

server.listen(0, '127.0.0.1', () => {
  http.request({ port: server.address().port, host: '127.0.0.1', method: 'POST' }, (res) => {
    res.resume();
    res.on('end', () => process.exit(0));
  }).end();
});
```

```
docker run --rm -v "$PWD":/src:ro node:24.22.1-alpine node /src/repro.mjs x
docker run --rm -v "$PWD":/src:ro node:24.22.1-alpine node /src/repro.mjs —
```

### How often does it reproduce? Is there a required condition?

100% in repeated runs. Required: chunked response (no `Content-Length`, the default for streamed writes), more than ~64 KiB written in total, and at least one non-ASCII string chunk (`—`, `é`, `😀` all trigger it). Using `Readable.from(...).pipe(res)` instead of the manual `write`/`drain` loop hangs the same way. An all-ASCII body of the same size completes.

### What is the expected behavior? Why is that the expected behavior?

The response completes (270,150 bytes in this repro), as it does on v24.21.0, v22.23.4, v25.9.0 and v26.8.2. When `res.write()` returns `false`, `'drain'` should be emitted once the buffered data has been flushed.

### What do you see instead?

```
v24.21.0 char="x" DONE writes=12000 drainWaits=5
v24.21.0 char="—" DONE writes=12000 drainWaits=5
v24.22.1 char="x" DONE writes=12000 drainWaits=4
v24.22.1 char="—" STUCK: writes=2967 drainWaits=1 writableLength=0 hwm=65536 corked=0 socketWritableLength=0
```

After the first `write()` that returns `false`, `'drain'` is never emitted, even though `res.writableLength` is 0, the socket's `writableLength` is 0 and nothing is corked. The server simply stops. Inspecting the raw socket, the bytes already sent are valid chunked framing (one complete ~64 KiB chunk), and no further data is written, so the client waits until its own timeout. With ASCII-only data the writes are coalesced into larger chunks and the response completes.

Same test with a plain-`pipe` variant (`Readable.from(strings).pipe(res)`, 4000 rows, 270 KB), one run per version, plus repeated runs on 24.22.x and 26.12.x:

| Node | ASCII `x` | non-ASCII `—`, `é`, `😀` |
|---|---|---|
| 22.23.4 | OK | OK |
| 24.21.0 | OK | OK |
| 24.22.0 | OK | **stalls after ~65 KB** |
| 24.22.1 | OK | **stalls after ~65 KB** |
| 25.9.0 | OK | OK |
| 26.7.0, 26.8.2 | OK | OK |
| 26.9.0, 26.10.0, 26.11.1, 26.12.0, 26.12.1 | OK | **stalls after ~65 KB** |

### Additional information

Not bisected. Possibly related: the chunked-write coalescing rework in `lib/_http_outgoing.js` (nodejs/node#64987, "http: coalesce chunked writes during auto-corking"), which is listed in the v24.22.0 changelog. I only know the regression window from the version table above.

We found this in production when a streamed JSON endpoint (`stream-json` piped into `res`) stopped completing after our base image moved from 24.21.0 to 24.22.0. Our endpoint returned an 844 KB body that included `—`, `–` and `’`. Replacing those three characters with `?` made it complete, and so did pinning to `node:24.21.0`. Rolling back the JSON stream library to an older major also made it complete on 24.22.0; we have not investigated why that changes the write pattern. The security releases from Oct 21 (22.23.4, 24.22.1, 26.12.1) do not change the behaviour.
